{"title":"1959–1966 IMO Longlist Problems/Czechoslovakia 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Resources\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Resources</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1959%E2%80%931966_IMO_Longlist_Problems/Czechoslovakia_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/b/deba155fc5baeab8c0343988e08e19043383721a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n &gt; 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> points in the plane such that no three of the points are collinear.  Does there exist a circle passing through (at least) 3 of the given points and not containing any other of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> points in its interior?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1959%E2%80%931966_IMO_Longlist_Problems/Czechoslovakia_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The answer is yes.\n</p><p>Since any finite set of at least three coplanar points is contained by a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Convex_hull\" title=\"Convex hull\">convex hull</a> with vertices in the set of points, we can select adjacent points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> on this convex hull.  Clearly all of the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/0/95055437bcca0a70c046144ca5d7eae724593b50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n-2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> points will lie on the same side of the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.  Of these other points, we select the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/1/1716e238f553f7dabd85afad448d78d505bc0ce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${C}$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> such that the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/21361fc8f29984fa3e95bfc0e578efdbce08c736.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> is maximized.  Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> satisfy the conditions of the problem, because if there were some point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> inside the circle, since it would be on the same side of line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/1/1716e238f553f7dabd85afad448d78d505bc0ce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${C}$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />, the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/1/8810a26f59e2c67b5f2ff052a7e2a294fef0a03e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADB$\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> would be greater than the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/21361fc8f29984fa3e95bfc0e578efdbce08c736.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, which is a contradiction.  Q.E.D.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_2\">Proof 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1959%E2%80%931966_IMO_Longlist_Problems/Czechoslovakia_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The problem statement is correct.\n</p><p>In any space, we have three points categorized by two \"edge\" points and one \"middle\" point. The \"middle\" point is always positioned between the two edge points. For any configuration for which not all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points are cyclic, one can choose two points that lie on the same circle and an external point that does not lie on the circle formed by the two points, with the caveat that there are no points to the left or right of the external point. For instance, assume a space <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> that consists of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points, not all concyclic. Then, Alfa (our little helper friend) can choose three points closest to the border of the space <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> such that:\n</p>\n<ul><li>The angle formed by the three points positioned at the middle point is maximal.</li>\n<li>The angle formed by the three points faces away from all other points.</li></ul>\n<p>We now prove that all such three positions of points work. Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> are the three points which satisfy this problem. The first part is to show all such configurations of three points exist, which is true by the definition of the problem, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />. Otherwise, if there existed a point that uniquely faces the \"largest\" angle, then say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/1/8810a26f59e2c67b5f2ff052a7e2a294fef0a03e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADB$\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> is larger than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/21361fc8f29984fa3e95bfc0e578efdbce08c736.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> in this case by the Interior Point Angle Inequality, which is contradictory.\n</p><p>Thus because such a configuration exists on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/3/ac39551d89b70c00e199e97d114569bd8893d5e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S \\in \\mathbb{U}_p \\ \\forall \\ \\mathbb{U}_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"98\" height=\"19\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/f/c6f13f215cb5a99511c181a61f941347c522b17c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{U}_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"22\" height=\"19\" /> is the universal set that satisfies the problem, all elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/f/c6f13f215cb5a99511c181a61f941347c522b17c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{U}_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"22\" height=\"19\" /> satisfy this set and thus the problem statement is correct. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1959%E2%80%931966_IMO_Longlist_Problems/Czechoslovakia_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=16193\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1959%E2%80%931966_IMO_Longlist_Problems\" title=\"1959–1966 IMO Longlist Problems\">1959&#8211;1966 IMO Longlist Problems</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018041331\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.015 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.025 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 152/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4699/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3620:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018041331 and revision id 291366. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}